<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    THE TREASURE CHESTS. One drops where the boss died every time a dungeon or
    tower floor is cleared (DungeonMapComponent.HandleCleared), tiered by the
    rank band: T1 F-C, T2 B-A, T3 S-SS, T4 SSS-Nation.

    They are ITEMS, deliberately: haulable, stockpileable, caravan-able, and
    openable anywhere - the right-click Open ceremony works exactly the same
    standing in the dungeon or back home a season later. Opening consumes the
    chest and pays out one forge-enhanced weapon or armor via the reel window
    (Window_TreasureReel); the odds and power tables live in TreasurePool.

    Grades are METALS - bronze, silver, gold, jade - which is a separate
    ladder from the item rarities the reel cards show. The defNames still read
    Plain/Fine/Grand/Mythic because renaming them would strand chests in
    existing saves; the label is what anyone actually sees.

    The open-lid texture is found by convention: the def's texPath + "_Open".
    All four have their own art now, so the base texPath here is only a
    fallback - a new grade that forgets its graphicData gets the bronze chest
    rather than a missing-texture square.
  -->

  <!--
    PARENTED TO Items, NOT Root, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON CHESTS ARE
    HAULABLE. A default stockpile does not accept everything - ThingFilter
    .SetFromPreset allows exactly eight categories (Foods, Manufactured,
    ResourcesRaw, Items, Buildings, Weapons, Apparel, BodyParts) and nothing
    else. A category hung directly off Root belongs to none of them, so no
    stockpile would take a chest, no haul job was ever generated, and the
    chests simply sat where they dropped forever.

    Items is the vanilla catch-all for miscellaneous goods and is on that list,
    so anything under it is hauled with no player setup at all. Keep any future
    IsekaiDungeons category under one of the eight, and check with
    Tools/verify_chests.py, which now fails the build if the chain does not
    reach an allowed root.
  -->
  <ThingCategoryDef>
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_Treasures</defName>
    <label>treasure chests</label>
    <parent>Items</parent>
  </ThingCategoryDef>

  <ThingDef Name="IsekaiTreasureChestBase" Abstract="True">
    <thingClass>IsekaiDungeons.TreasureChest</thingClass>
    <category>Item</category>
    <label>treasure chest</label>
    <selectable>true</selectable>
    <altitudeLayer>Item</altitudeLayer>
    <alwaysHaulable>true</alwaysHaulable>
    <tickerType>Never</tickerType>
    <rotatable>false</rotatable>
    <stackLimit>1</stackLimit>
    <tradeability>Sellable</tradeability>
    <pathCost>14</pathCost>
    <thingCategories>
      <li>IsekaiDungeons_Treasures</li>
    </thingCategories>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>150</MaxHitPoints>
      <Mass>8</Mass>
      <Flammability>0</Flammability>
      <DeteriorationRate>0.5</DeteriorationRate>
      <Beauty>4</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Treasures/TreasureChest_Bronze</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>(1.3,1.3)</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <drawGUIOverlay>false</drawGUIOverlay>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="IsekaiTreasureChestBase">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_TreasureChestPlain</defName>
    <label>bronze chest</label>
    <description>A dungeon's parting gift. Something is sealed inside - a weapon or a piece of armor, reinforced and possibly runed - and the only way to learn which is to open it. Anyone can open it, anywhere, and it is exactly as portable as it looks.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>400</MarketValue>
    </statBases>
    <comps>
      <li Class="IsekaiDungeons.CompProperties_TreasureChest">
        <tier>1</tier>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="IsekaiTreasureChestBase">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_TreasureChestFine</defName>
    <label>silver chest</label>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Treasures/TreasureChest_Silver</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>(1.3,1.3)</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <description>A parting gift from a dungeon that meant it. The gear sealed in a chest of this grade arrives well reinforced, and runes are more likely than not.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>950</MarketValue>
    </statBases>
    <comps>
      <li Class="IsekaiDungeons.CompProperties_TreasureChest">
        <tier>2</tier>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="IsekaiTreasureChestBase">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_TreasureChestGrand</defName>
    <label>gold chest</label>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Treasures/TreasureChest_Gold</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>(1.3,1.3)</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <description>Sealed by something that guarded it seriously. What comes out of a chest of this grade would anchor an armory.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>1900</MarketValue>
    </statBases>
    <comps>
      <li Class="IsekaiDungeons.CompProperties_TreasureChest">
        <tier>3</tier>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="IsekaiTreasureChestBase">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_TreasureChestMythic</defName>
    <label>jade chest</label>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Treasures/TreasureChest_Jade</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
      <!--
        NOT 1.3 like the others, and this is deliberate. The jade texture spends
        a quarter of its canvas on wings, so the CASKET inside it is drawn at
        75% and at the shared 1.3 it would render a quarter smaller than a gold
        chest sitting beside it - the wings would have cost the chest its size
        instead of adding to it.

        1.74 is measured, not guessed: the jade casket inks 136px of the 256
        texture against gold's 182, and 1.3 * 182/136 = 1.74 puts both caskets
        at 0.924 tiles wide. Anchored on WIDTH because the box is the widest
        part of both; the heights differ only because gold's finial is taller
        in proportion than jade's coronet. The full wingspan then covers about
        1.6 tiles, which is the overhang the wings are supposed to have.

        If the wings are ever resized, RE-MEASURE: Tools/measure_drawsize.py
        prints the value.
      -->
      <drawSize>(1.74,1.74)</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <description>There are not many of these, because there are not many places that can produce one. Whatever is inside was worth sealing behind the strongest thing a dungeon could field.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MarketValue>3600</MarketValue>
    </statBases>
    <comps>
      <li Class="IsekaiDungeons.CompProperties_TreasureChest">
        <tier>4</tier>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>

  <JobDef>
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_OpenTreasureChest</defName>
    <driverClass>IsekaiDungeons.JobDriver_OpenTreasureChest</driverClass>
    <reportString>opening TargetA.</reportString>
    <allowOpportunisticPrefix>false</allowOpportunisticPrefix>
  </JobDef>

</Defs>
